other contributing factors, in a statement about the company\u2019s fourth-quarter 2022 earnings results.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 175, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 51, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">One of the world\u2019s largest insurance companies, Prudential Financial operates in over 40 countries, offering services such as life insurance, mutual funds, and annuities. The company aims to have net-zero emissions across its primary global home office operations by 2050. It has also bounced back from early pandemic losses that led to the company losing money in 2020. A quarterly dividend increase of 4% in the first quarter of 2022 represents Prudential Financial\u2019s 14th consecutive annual dividend boost, and the company plans to return $11 billion of capital to shareholders by the end of 2023.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 178, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The insurance giant felt the squeeze of the pandemic in 2020; Prudential\u2019s revenues fell 12%, while it posted a net loss of $374 million (compared with profits of nearly $4.2 billion in 2019). But chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey has talked up plans to invest up to $10 billion into \u201chigher-growth, less market-sensitive\u201d businesses, as well as a cost-cutting target of $750 million by the end of 2023. One bright spot remains PGIM, the insurer\u2019s massive investment management arm, which saw its assets under management (AUM) swell 13% to nearly $1.5 trillion at the end of 2020.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The insurance giant felt the squeeze of the pandemic in 2020; Prudential\u2019s revenues fell 12%, while it posted a net loss of $374 million (compared with profits of nearly $4.2 billion in 2019). But chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey has talked up plans to invest up to $10 billion into \u201chigher-growth, less market-sensitive\u201d businesses, as well as a cost-cutting target of $750 million by the end of 2023. One bright spot remains PGIM, the insurer\u2019s massive investment management arm, which saw its assets under management (AUM) swell 13% to nearly $1.5 trillion at the end of 2020.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 55, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The insurance giant felt the squeeze of the pandemic in 2020; Prudential\u2019s revenues fell 12%, while it posted a net loss of $374 million (compared with profits of nearly $4.2 billion in 2019). But chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey has talked up plans to invest up to $10 billion into \u201chigher-growth, less market-sensitive\u201d businesses, as well as a cost-cutting target of $750 million by the end of 2023. One bright spot remains PGIM, the insurer\u2019s massive investment management arm, which saw its assets under management (AUM) swell 13% to nearly $1.5 trillion at the end of 2020.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The insurance giant felt the squeeze of the pandemic in 2020; Prudential\u2019s revenues fell 12%, while it posted a net loss of $374 million (compared with profits of nearly $4.2 billion in 2019). But chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey has talked up plans to invest up to $10 billion into \u201chigher-growth, less market-sensitive\u201d businesses, as well as a cost-cutting target of $750 million by the end of 2023. One bright spot remains PGIM, the insurer\u2019s massive investment management arm, which saw its assets under management (AUM) swell 13% to nearly $1.5 trillion at the end of 2020.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 167, "itemDescription": "The insurance giant saw both revenues and earnings climb nearly 3% year-over-year in 2019, with chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey talking up Prudential\u2019s ability to \u201cdrive greater efficiency across our operations\u201d in its year-end earnings report. The company returned roughly $4 billion to shareholders in 2019 and says it\u2019s taking actions to mitigate the negative impact of low interest rates. Meanwhile, at PGIM\u2014Prudential\u2019s massive money management arm\u2014assets under management (AUM) climbed 13%, to more than $1.3 trillion at the end of 2019."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 53, "itemDescription": "The insurance giant saw both revenues and earnings climb nearly 3% year-over-year in 2019, with chairman and CEO Charles Lowrey talking up Prudential\u2019s ability to \u201cdrive greater efficiency across our operations\u201d in its year-end earnings report. The company returned roughly $4 billion to shareholders last year and says it\u2019s taking actions to mitigate the negative impact of low interest rates. Meanwhile, PGIM\u2014Prudential\u2019s massive money management arm\u2014saw its assets under management (AUM) climb 13%, to more than $1.3 trillion at the end of 2019."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500",